Two changes are needed.

(a) Make re-applying the same fog a no-op. In look.js `fog.set`:
```js
const o = lookFogOverride(v);
const cur = win.__environFogOverride;
if (o && cur && cur.option === o.option && Number.isFinite(cur.fogRgb)) return { ok: true, value: o.name, kept: true };
win.__environFogOverride = o;
return { ok: true, value: o ? o.name : "none", changed: true };
```
This keeps the existing object, so environOverride.sync sees the same identity and does not reset the transition. Re-prepare, retakes and later shots at the same site then keep the fog already established.

(b) When the fog really changes (the first take with fog, or a different option), let it settle before the take. In film/index.js prepare, after look.apply, if `lookResult.applied.fog?.changed` and `window.__environOverrideState` exists, poll it until `fogT >= 1` (about 20-25 s wall). Use a timeout of about 40 s and report the result in the prepare summary. The alternative is to add `environRetail=off` to buildFilmUrl in tools/film/lib/session.mjs so the fog snaps on. Note that this also drops the retail ambient tint and the sky veil, and gives near = 0.1·far, so the look changes. Whichever option is chosen, document it in look.js and add a film_runtime_world/look test proving that a repeated apply keeps the identity of `__environFogOverride`.

