```

Also wrap the call to `goAndWait` in a per-site try/catch that rethrows USAGE errors. The teleport itself can throw, for example on an EVAL_TIMEOUT from the portal probe. Any other error becomes `failures.push({shotId, siteId, stage: "site", message})` for each of that site's shots, followed by `continue`. With this change, one site's setup error is recorded instead of aborting the run, and the summary is always returned.

In scene3d/film/index.js resolveTake, entity/player branch, replace `if (subj.moving === true) ctx.subjectAt = liveSubject(ref, ov);` with a liveness test that also covers the player as actor. take.actor may be one cue or an array of cues, the same shapes take_player.actorCuesOf accepts:

```js
const cues = Array.isArray(take.actor) ? take.actor : isObj(take.actor) ? [take.actor] : [];
const actorMoves = cues.some((c) => isObj(c) && (c.moves === true || (c.moveTo !== undefined && c.moveTo !== null)));
const isLive = subj.moving === true || (kind === "player" && (actorMoves || cap.mode === "realtime"));
if (isLive) ctx.subjectAt = liveSubject(ref, ov);
```

Then report `moving: isLive` in subjectInfo. Optionally, schema.mjs normShot (or compile.mjs) can also stamp `subject.moving = true` on a `{kind:'player'}` subject when the actor moves. checkSubject would then need to accept a boolean `moving` on player subjects too. Stepped takes with a player who does not move keep the static subject, so locked replays stay deterministic. As the claim notes, tracking rigs that sample the subject only when the trajectory is built need their own fix before the camera path follows the walk. This change alone already fixes the sensors, the pilot and the take metrics.

In scene3d/film/index.js, add a helper that reads the shared sky snapshot instead of calling wasm getSkyState() again:

```js
function sunCtx() {
  try {
    const s = live.skyLightingController && live.skyLightingController._lastState;
    if (s && Number.isFinite(s.dirHeading) && Number.isFinite(s.dirPitch)) {
      const D = Math.PI / 180;
      return { h: s.dirHeading * D, p: s.dirPitch * D };
    }
  } catch (_) {}
  return null;
}
```

SkyState values are degrees, heading clockwise from +Y north, pointing toward the sun (see particle_fx.js:595 and sun_direction.js:49; the pkg d.ts "radians" comment is wrong). This matches the solver/classic convention.

At the end of resolveTake, when `!ctx.indoor`, add `const sun = sunCtx(); if (sun) ctx.sun = sun;`. usableSun() already drops indoor and below-horizon suns. scout inherits the sun through `...resolved.ctx`.

Also make the degradation visible. In solver rankCandidates/scoreCandidate (or in index.js scout), when intent.light is set and no usable ctx.sun exists, add a reason or warning such as "light intent ignored: no sun (indoors/night/unknown)". The director then knows that 'back'/'rim' had no effect.

Add a unit test that sets intent.light='back' with ctx.sun and asserts the top candidate's lightAngleDeg is at least 150. Add another that asserts the warning appears when there is no sun.

